   {"review":"Last week was a memory-and-foundry breakout with software left behind — the AI supply chain kept bidding hardware while the application layer sold off, and we called the split right. This is our first weekly on this ledger: nothing settled, the ledger carries zero commitments and zero open calls, so no Brier score exists yet. But our themes and focus list had a week. The sharpest thing we got right was leaning into the hardware complex: sector:Memory/Storage led every equity row and sector:Foundry was second, both with all members up versus SPY — the read that El Nino-adjacent capex and AI buildout would keep flowing into physical supply held cleanly. sector:Computer/GPU confirmed the same, all six names green. Where we would have been wrong is anyone who treated software as one bloc: sector:Cybersecurity and sector:Software/SaaS were the two worst rows on the board, both deeply negative versus a flat benchmark, while sector:Cloud/Hyperscaler and sector:Foundation-Model-Proxy barely moved. The application layer did not participate in the hardware bid — a real dispersion, not a broad risk-off. Cross-asset backed the constructive tone: rates.long eased, credit spreads tightened a touch and vol dropped, none of which argued for the software selloff, so the weakness there was rotation, not fear. On the theme we entered mid-week, theme:el-nino-ag-2026 was the single best-performing basket we track on a one-week and four-week basis, leading the rotation table outright — the entry timing looks vindicated so far, though it has been live only since 2026-09-06 and its since-entered excess is still zero. Nothing to walk back; plenty still unproven.","outlook":"Item by item over what the week actually did. Memory and foundry leadership: I expect it to CONTINUE. Both baskets led on strength that was broad across members, not one name, and MU reports 2026-09-30 as the next hardware tell — nothing before then reverses it. Wrong if the Foundry basket's member breadth flips to a majority red versus SPY next week. The software/cyber weakness: I lean CONTINUE rather than snap-back, because the two worst rows fell against easing rates and tightening credit — this was rotation out, and rotation rarely mean-reverts in one week. The near catalyst is ORCL and ADBE both reporting post-close 2026-09-10; a clean ORCL cloud beat is the one print that could REVERSE the Cloud/Hyperscaler drift, and an ADBE beat could stabilise Software/SaaS. Wrong if either gaps up and drags its basket green. The macro backdrop: rates.long, credit and vol all moved the risk-friendly way, and the dominant event is FOMC 9/16, priced as a hike above even odds. This is the week's real hinge — a hold surprise STRENGTHENS every long-duration hardware and software row; a confirmed hike leaves the rates row where it is and keeps the pressure on the rate-sensitive application names. Wrong on the constructive read if credit spreads widen into the meeting. The ag theme: I expect it to CONTINUE leading given it topped the rotation table on both one- and four-week windows, but the honest caveat is it has no since-entered track record yet — one week of confirming price does not make the thesis. Wrong if the basket underperforms SPY next week, the first crack in a kill line that is nowhere near armed. FX (broad dollar) firmed slightly and is stale to late August; I would not build anything on it until it refreshes. Net: stay with hardware, respect the software rotation, and let 9/10 earnings and the FOMC do the deciding.","catalysts":"ADBE earnings post-close 2026-09-10, implied move 6.9333%. ORCL earnings post-close 2026-09-10, implied move 9.8619% — both land the same evening and are the week's near tells for the software and cloud rows. FOMC 9/16, priced HIKE 55.7% against a 3.75-4.00% prior — the macro hinge for every rate-sensitive row. Further out and admitted to the list: MU earnings post-close 2026-09-30, implied move 12.373%; then a dense October tape — ASML 2026-1